<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="subsection-infinity" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Infinity
  </title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      <!-- Text before the first subdivision. -->
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <definition xml:id="definition-extended-real-number">
    <title>
      Extended real number
    </title>
    
    <idx>
      extended real number
    </idx>
    
    <idx>
      infinity
    </idx>
    
    <notation>
      <usage>
        <m> \overline{ \R } </m>
      </usage>
      
      <description>
        <p>
          the set of extended real numbers
        </p>
      </description>
    </notation>
    
    <notation>
      <usage>
        <m> \infty </m>
      </usage>
      
      <description>
        <p>
          positive infinity
        </p>
      </description>
    </notation>
    
    <notation>
      <usage>
        <m> -\infty </m>
      </usage>
      
      <description>
        <p>
          negative infinity
        </p>
      </description>
    </notation>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        An <term> extended real number </term> is either a real number or one of two <term> infinite </term> elements <m> \infty </m> or <m> -\infty </m>. The set of extended real numbers is denoted <m> \overline{ \R } = \R \cup \set{ \infty , -\infty } </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        The standard ordering <m> \leq </m> on the real numbers <m> \R </m> is extended to a total order on <m> \overline{ \R } </m> by requiring <m> -\infty \leq x \leq \infty </m> for all real numbers <m> x \in \R </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  
  <definition xml:id="definition-interval">
    <title>
      Interval
    </title>
    
    <idx>
      interval
    </idx>
    
    <notation>
      <usage>
        <m>  </m>
      </usage>
      
      <description>
        <p>
          
        </p>
      </description>
    </notation>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> a , b \in \overline{ \R } </m> be extended real numbers so that <m> a \leq b </m>.
        
        <ol>
          <li>
            <title>
              Closed interval
            </title>
            
            <p>
              The <term> closed interval </term> <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m> from <m> a </m> to <m> b </m> is the subset of the extended real numbers <m> \overline{ \R } </m> defined by
              
              <md>
                \clint{ a }{ b } = \setmid{ x \in \overline{ \R } }{ a \leq x \leq b }
              </md>.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <title>
              Open interval
            </title>
            
            <p>
              The <term> open interval </term> <m> \opint{ a }{ b } </m> from <m> a </m> to <m> b </m> is the subset of the extended real numbers <m> \overline{ \R } </m> defined by
              
              <md>
                \opint{ a }{ b } = \setmid{ x \in \overline{ \R } }{ a \lt x \lt b }
              </md>.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <title>
              Half-closed, half-open interval
            </title>
            
            <p>
              The <term> half-closed, half-open interval </term> <m> \clopint{ a }{ b } </m> from <m> a </m> to <m> b </m> is the subset of the extended real numbers <m> \overline{ \R } </m> defined by
              
              <md>
                \clopint{ a }{ b } = \setmid{ x \in \overline{ \R } }{ a \leq x \lt b }
              </md>.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <title>
              Closed interval
            </title>
            
            <p>
              The <term> half-open, half-closed interval </term> <m> \opclint{ a }{ b } </m> from <m> a </m> to <m> b </m> is the subset of the extended real numbers <m> \overline{ \R } </m> defined by
              
              <md>
                \opclint{ a }{ b } = \setmid{ x \in \overline{ \R } }{ a \lt x \leq b }
              </md>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  
  <example xml:id="example-intervals">
    <title>
      Intervals
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        The following are examples of intervals:
        
        <ul>
          <li>
            <title>
              (Extended) real numbers
            </title>
            
            <p>
              The real numbers <m> \R </m> and the extended real numbers <m> \overline{ \R } </m> are open and closed intervals, respectively; concretely, <m> \R = \opint{ -\infty }{ \infty } </m> and <m> \overline{ \R } = \clint{ -\infty }{ \infty } </m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <title>
              Dedekind cuts
            </title>
            
            <p>
              Given a real number <m> x \in \R </m>, the Dedekind cut <m> X </m> corresponding to <m> x </m> is <m> X = \Q \cap \opint{ -\infty }{ x } </m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <theorem xml:id="theorem-suprema-and-infima">
    <title>
      Suprema and infima
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Any subset of the extended real numbers <m> \overline{ \R } </m> has a supremum in <m> \overline{ \R } </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <p>
        Let <m> U \subseteq \overline{ \R } </m> be a subset of the extended real numbers <m> \R </m>. If <m> \infty \in U </m>, then <m> \sup \of{ U } = \max \of{ U } = \infty </m>. If <m> \infty \notin U </m> and <m> U \cap \R </m> is not bounded from above, then
        
        <md>
          \sup \of{ U } = \sup \of{ U \cap \R } = \infty
        </md>.
        
        If <m> \infty \notin U </m> and <m> U \cap \R \neq \emptyset </m> is bounded from above, then
        
        <md>
          \sup \of{ U } = \sup \of{ U \cap \R }
        </md>.
        
        Finally, if <m> \infty \notin U </m> and <m> U \cap \R = \emptyset </m>, then <m> \sup \of{ U } = -\infty </m>.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>

  <conclusion>
    <idx>
      indeterminate form
    </idx>
    
    <p>
      We note that the binary operations of addition and multiplication do not fully extend to the set <m> \overline{ \R } </m> of extended real numbers. Indeed, there are several <term> indeterminate forms </term> like <m> \infty + \of{ -\infty } </m> and <m> 0 \cdot \infty </m> which cannot be consistently resolved.
    </p>
  </conclusion>
</subsection>

